# Build this with ufbt from inside this folder. The result is a .fal you copy
# to your Flipper at:
#
#     SD Card/apps_data/fake_chip_detector/tests/
#
# The app creates that folder the first time it runs, so plug the Flipper in
# and open Fake Chip Detector once before you go looking for it.

# ufbt reads a plugin's requires[0] as its parent application and looks that
# name up in the manifests it has loaded -- and it loads exactly one, the file
# you are reading. Without this stub the build stops with "Missing application
# manifest for 'fake_chip_detector'".
#
# METAPACKAGE is the type to use because ufbt does not build it: only PLUGIN,
# EXTERNAL, MENUEXTERNAL and EXTSETTINGS become artifacts. So this declares the
# parent exists and produces nothing. Nothing here affects the .fal -- what
# actually decides whether the app will run the file is the appid and version
# in the descriptor at the bottom of live_mypart.c.
App(
    appid="fake_chip_detector",
    name="Fake Chip Detector",
    apptype=FlipperAppType.METAPACKAGE,
)

# apptype=PLUGIN is what makes ufbt emit a .fal instead of a .fap. A plugin
# manifest must declare `requires` and must NOT set `stack_size` -- it runs on
# a thread the host app already owns.
App(
    appid="live_test_mypart",
    name="MYPART live test",
    apptype=FlipperAppType.PLUGIN,
    entry_point="live_test_mypart_plugin_ep",
    requires=["fake_chip_detector"],
    sources=["live_mypart.c"],
    fap_category="GPIO",
    fap_version="1.0",
    fap_author="you",
    fap_description="Live test for the MYPART sensor.",
)
